# Drupal contribution workspace

Shared guidance for Claude Code and Codex. Read this file before issue work.

## What this project is

A **Drupal 11 contribution workbench** — a local environment for contributing patches and MRs to Drupal.org contrib modules. There is no custom application code. All work targets modules under `{{DRUPAL_WEBROOT}}/modules/contrib/`.

The entry point for any contribution task is **`/drupal-issue-start <url>`** — give it an issue URL and it loads prior context, fetches live state, and waits for direction. From there it delegates to `drupal-issue-agent` for review and implementation work, `drupal-e2e-tester` for the dedicated test phase (PHPUnit + Playwright browser e2e), or `drupal-issue-catchup` to sync after time away.

---

## Global Rule — Report Before Acting

Present findings before requesting approval for code changes, dependency changes, commits, or pushes. Reading, fetching, automatic recon (clone/checkout/fork remote), and issue-record maintenance follow their documented procedures without an extra gate. Respect the scope of the user's approval; request a new decision when the next phase exceeds it. Never answer a delegated agent's approval question for the human. Draft public comments for the human to post.

## Runtime conventions

The active client's root instruction file supplies `<skills-root>`: `.claude/skills` for Claude Code or `.agents/skills` for Codex. Resolve this once, alongside the site context, and pass it through all agent handoffs. Shell examples using `<skills-root>` must substitute the concrete path before execution. Discover external skills by their installed `SKILL.md` path; never assume a user-global location.

Procedures use `/skill-name` as notation. In Claude Code invoke `/skill-name`; in Codex invoke `$skill-name` or explicitly load that skill by name. Agent names denote delegated roles, not slash commands. Use the client's native subagent tools for Nora, Wren, Milo, and Sage. Pass the complete context and resume point; relay `[PAUSE — awaiting user decision]` reports to the human. If native custom roles are unavailable but subagents work, pass the full procedure from `.drupal-contrib/agents/<name>.md` to a fresh subagent. If delegation is unavailable, report the missing capability. Never label the implementing agent's checks as independent testing.

Role tool lists in Markdown describe Claude capabilities; Codex uses its native equivalents. A report-only tester may create test artifacts and exercise the site but must not edit module code. These role boundaries are workflow instructions, not a filesystem sandbox guarantee.

If a nested delegation is unavailable (for example Nora cannot spawn Milo at the client's nesting limit), return a `[DELEGATE — <agent-name>]` handoff with the full inputs and resume phase. The main conversation runs that role as a sibling subagent, then resumes the caller with its report. This is a routing request, not user approval; relay any missing approval through the existing `[PAUSE]` protocol first.

Both clients share `issues/`; avoid concurrent writers to the same issue record or module checkout. Use separate worktrees for concurrent code work and serialize record updates. Disclosures name the actual assistant(s) that contributed, including work inherited from the record.

