## Issue tracking system

Every issue worked on has a directory at `issues/<nid>/`. These directories survive across sessions — they are the long-term memory for contribution work.

```
issues/
└── 3456789/
    ├── README.md         ← persistent record: summary, related issues, work log, human notes
    ├── history.md        ← archived Work Log sessions older than the 3 most recent (only once it exists)
    ├── comments/         ← drafted Drupal.org / GitLab comments (markdown files)
    ├── screenshots/      ← browser screenshots captured during issue work and e2e test runs
    └── e2e/              ← generated Playwright artifacts from the drupal-e2e-tester phase
```

The README is the **long-term memory** for each issue. It persists across sessions. All agents read it at the start and write to it at the end. If related issue records exist (e.g. `issues/3001/README.md`), agents read those too. The README only ever holds the 3 most recent Work Log sessions — `issue-record-update` moves anything older to `history.md` so routine reads stay bounded; agents read `history.md` only when genuinely digging for older context, not as a matter of course.

### Key workflow rules

1. **Start every issue session with `/drupal-issue-start <url>`** — it loads the record, fetches live state, and presents a report before any work begins.
2. After every session, **`/issue-record-update <nid>`** logs what was done (no confirmation prompt — writes directly).
3. To catch up after time away, use **`/drupal-issue-catchup <nid>`** instead of re-running issue-start.
4. Screenshots go in `issues/<nid>/screenshots/` via **`/issue-record-screenshot`**, using the active client's connected browser integration or an existing Playwright session.
5. Drafted comments are saved to `issues/<nid>/comments/` via the **`drupalorg-comment-format`** skill.

